A||||9||GT Bastard||Need some advice on my motor build-up....||||11||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||07-03-2000||06:13 PM||||Ok, I've got a good 2.8 motor out of a running car to work with.  Only problem it has is bad rings.  However, I want to make this engine something special.  I'm planning on having it hot tanked and bored in a couple days. I'm going to order a 3.1 crank and make it a stroker motor.  Here's my choices...  The car is already setup for nitrous(1FST2M6's old car) so I'm thinking I might like to use dished pistons to handle the forced induction.  OR....  I might go naturally aspirated and get myself some flat-top pistons.  What do you guys think?  The friend that's helping me complete this project has done this swap in several Fiero's already, so it's just a matter of getting the pieces together.  Anybody tried getting pistons through Summit Racing?  I'm open to suggestions.||||reg||11
Z||000001||Taijiguy||07-03-2000||07:39 PM||||I haven't ordered pistons from them, but I did order a manifold, and their service was impeccable. I ordered it over the net, and it went very smooth.||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||07-03-2000||08:02 PM||||Your logic is sound on the choice of pistons. I'd use forged pistons, knowing this is going to be a high performance engine. It's expensive now, but worth it in the long run.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 07-03-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||GT Bastard||07-03-2000||08:12 PM||||Are Forged pistons really necessary on a natually aspirated motor?  Is the primary reason for going forged pistons forced induction?  How bout some ideas for the camshaft...  The car already has some major fuel system mods, headers, extrude honed intake, so it can handle a hot cam.  Any recommendations?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Megafiero||07-03-2000||09:14 PM||||Forged pistons are not necessary on a natually aspirated motor, but can be a good idea.  Most the time they are used on a motor that will have forced induction or nitrous,  but when pushing up the HP a good deal over stock, it never herts to play it safe,  and this will also leave you open to further upgrades down the road, without rebuilding the motor again.<p>[This message has been edited by Megafiero (edited 07-03-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||07-03-2000||09:15 PM||||Get the engine balanced; it is critical if you want a smooth running engine that lasts a long time.||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||07-03-2000||10:25 PM||||Yeah, what Mega said.||||reg||1
Z||000007||webbee||07-03-2000||10:41 PM||||I agree with 88fiero you get a lot from the balancing gimmick. Ditto port & polish. I also like counterweighted cranks. Don't put too much cam to it, if it's a streeter.<P>Summet is good for mail order. Never bought pistons but lot's of other stuff. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||07-04-2000||07:00 AM||||Pocket porting is good, polishing doesn't do much. I've never seen a crankshaft that didn't have counterweights. Summit's ok, but I like JEGS High Performance better.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Eric||07-04-2000||04:27 PM||||You should buy forged pistons.  You can't get flat top hypereutectic cast pistons, so your only option is forged.  The silvolites and the federal mogul pistons have a slight dish, but still give 8.5:1 compression ratio.  So a flat top forged would be close to 10.0:1, which would be perfect for a naturally aspirated engine with maybe a little juice now and again with octane booster.  They will need to be custom made pistons.  I believe JE will make them for you for roughly $800.  There are other piston manufacturers, but I think JE is about the best you can do (you won't save much money going to someone else either).  For comparison, the hypereutectic cast pistons cost $90 a set.  They're good pistons, but I wouldn't want to go forced induction with those or atleast not until Shaun puts a few miles on his aluminum 3.1 nitrous motor with the Federal Moguls.  After he gives the thumbs up, I might try a 50 shot just for fun.<P>You should buy the GM performace crank.  It's like $400 but you don't want to use a remanufactured one instead.  It comes from the factory already balanced, but get it balanced again just to be sure.  I think Hypnotise has a steel crank, but he's also going real big ($$$) and you really don't need a steel crank without four bolt mains.  Get everything on the rotating assembly balanced.  You can reuse the old rods just get them magnafluxed and shot peened and use new ARP rod bolts.<P>Buy the GM hipo cam.  It's about the biggest your computer can handle (although Shaun is trying 1.6 ratio rocker arms, we'll see how that goes too).  It's also $50 cheaper than the same one made by compcams.<P>All the part numbers are in one of my old 3.1 threads "This is what's in my 3.2" or something like that.||||reg||1
